course: dictionary
lesson: 13581
title: Incise: cutting with care
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To incise means to cut carefully into a hard or smooth surface, such as stone, wood or skin. It can also mean to carve a pattern or letters into something. «The stonemason incised her name into the grave.» The word carefully tells you the cut is neat and planned, not a rough chop.
explain: Incise is a formal word. Everyday words for the same action are «cut» and «carve». Try swapping: «The stonemason carved her name into the grave» still makes sense, so carve is a close partner for incise.
explain: Use the context to check your meaning. «The surgeon incised the skin before the operation.» A surgeon makes a careful, planned cut, not a messy one. Ask yourself: what does a careful person do to stone, wood or skin? That is the clue to the meaning.
fact: To incise means to cut carefully into a surface.
fact: Incise can also mean to carve a pattern or letters into something.
fact: «Incised» is the past tense: «The potter incised a pattern yesterday.»
fact: In art and history, you may find patterns incised on pottery and letters incised in stone.
fact: In medicine, a doctor incises the skin during an operation.
exercise: «The potter incised a zigzag pattern into the wet clay.» What does incised mean here? | Broken into small pieces | Painted over with bright colours | Washed off with water | *Carved or cut a pattern into
exercise: «The mason incised the name on the stone.» Which everyday word could replace incise here? | *carve | sing | bounce | freeze
exercise: Which sentence uses incise correctly? | The sun incised the sky at noon. | *The doctor incised the skin carefully. | The children incised the rain with a song. | The dog incised a bone for supper.
test: «The doctor incised the skin neatly, so the cut was small and clean.» What does incised mean? | Tickled | *Cut carefully | Rubbed gently | Stitched closed
test: Incised letters are letters that are... | Written in sand | *Cut into stone or wood | Sung aloud | Painted on paper
exam: «The artist incised a pattern into the wooden box.» Which word is the closest everyday partner for incise? | jump | *carve | sing | sleep
